# Power BI Sales Performance Dashboard

## Overview
This project analyzes the performance of sales through interactive power bi dashboard to show revenue trends, customer behavior, product effectiveness and regional performance. The goal is to identify What drives revenue, Where performance lags and how the business can enhance efficiency and growth.

## Business Objective
Analyze sales performance across products, time periods, regions, categories and customers to know revenue drivers and actionable opportunities to enhance revenue and average order value (AOV).

## Data Source
The dataset contains sales transactions including:
- product and category information
- Order details
- customer and regional data
- Order quantity and revenue

Data was Cleaned, modeled and visualized using Power BI and SQL.

Tools Used:
- Power BI
- Dax
- Data modeling
- Data Visualization
- SQL

## Key KPIs
- Total Orders: 9,994
- Total Revenue: $287K
- Average Order Value : 57.26

## Performance Summary
- Sales Performance is generated because of the volume with many orders but relatively low AOV
- Most Revenue is generated from small number of high performance technology products
- Strong seasonality is observed with revenue peaking in December and declining sharply in January
- Performance varies significantly across regions, categories and customers

## Key Drivers 

### Product Performance
- Best Product (Sales & Revenue): canon imageclass 2200 advanced copier
- Worst Product (Sales): Eureka Disposable Bags for sanitaire vibra Groomer Upright vacuum
- Worst Product (Revenue): Cubify Cubex 3D Printer (Double head Print)

High priced technology products generate most of the revenue, While niche and accessory products contribut mminimally.

### Time Based Trends
- Best Month in Revenue: December
- Worst Month in Revenue: January

Revenue increases during year-end demand and declines during the post holiday period.

### Category Performance
- Best Region: West
- Worst Region: Central

The west region out performs due to stronger demand and a higher concentration of high value customers.

### Customer Behavior
- Top customer in Revenue: Tamara Chand
- Lowest customer in Revenue: Cindy stewart
- Most Orders: William Brown
- Fewest Orders: Ricardo Emerson, Anthony O'Donnell, Jocasta Rupert, Carl Jackson, Lela Donovan

Revenue is generated by a small group of high value customers, While frequent buyers contribure the most by order volume.

## Business Risks identified
- The most of dependence is on small number of high performance products
- Low average order value can constrain revenue growth
- Underperforming categories and regions can increase inventory and operatiional costs

## Recommendations

### Product Strategy
- Support high performance technology products
- Bundle low selling accessories with popular items
- Reposition low revenue niche products

### Revenue & Pricing Strategy
- Increase AOV by develop bundling and cross selling stratigies
- Introduce volume based pricing for business customers

### Seasonal Strategy
- Develop targeted promotions to reduce january revenue decline
- Prepare inventory and marketing campaigns ahead of the December peak

### Regional Strategy
- Replicate successful West region practices in central
- Apply targeted promotions and review logistics performance

### Customer Strategy
- Introduce loyalty or Vip programs for customers with high revenue
- Upsell frequent customers using specialized offers

## Business value Added
- The convertion of sales data into business insights
- The identification of the risk places of revenue and growth opportunities
- Supporting the decisions that depends on data across product, pricing and regional strategies
- Demonstrating strong analytical and business thinking

